WEBVTT

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.360
<b>Adrift</b>

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:24.130
We're... really alive, aren't we?

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.170
Hey... I'm asking you.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:30.340
I'm not "you."

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.040
Then tell me your name.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.840
Before asking someone's name,
you should first introduce your—

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.020
Oh, for crying out loud!
Then let's do introductions.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:38.680
What?

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:40.210
I'll go first.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:41.700
I'm Suzumori Asuka.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.730
I'm in Class C, and I'm on both the
basketball team and the track team.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.400
Nice to meet you!

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:48.730
I'll go next.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.890
I'm Kujou Shion from Class 2-B.

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:56.660
I like to play piano, and I have
a dog named Arnold.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:58.370
Pleased to meet you.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:00:59.740
Um...

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:03.230
I'm from Class A. My name is Amatani Mutsu.

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:06.170
My hobbies are reading and cooking... Oh!

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:09.750
And I've held on to the number one spot
in my class since I was a freshman!

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:12.010
Also, I've never been late once!

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:14.220
And I'm confident in my ability
to memorize things!

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.280
I also know a lot about astronomy!

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.340
Really... Okay. Next.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:20.520
Onishima Homare.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:21.730
Class F.

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:25.010
Huh? That's it?

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:27.230
You don't have anything else?
Something to make people like you?

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.660
Something to make people like me?

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.610
I don't know if this counts, but...

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:35.340
Being stranded like this...

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:37.740
This isn't my first time.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:43.680
We're stranded?

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:54.710
<b>Are You Lost?</b>

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:57.510
<b>Are You Lost?</b>

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:49.230
<b>Twenty Hours Earlier</b>

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:56.320
<i>Listen closely, Homare.</i>

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:59.780
When you face your opponent, make sure
to stand ready with your blade hidden.

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:04.260
Homare, you should never eat slugs raw.

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:08.580
Moose testicles are a rare and valuable
cut of meat that can be eaten raw.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:10.710
But don't expect much in regard to flavor.

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:14.350
Papa...

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:15.680
B*lls stink!

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:18.640
B*lls stink!

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:20.470
Papa...

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:21.520
Huh?

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:23.300
Papa?

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:24.100
B*lls?

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:24.640
Huh?

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:25.680
Oh! She's awake.

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:27.900
I-I'm fine...

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.270
How is this fine? We got washed away!

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:33.490
It's a miracle we survived,

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:36.050
but how long do we have to wait to be rescued?

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:38.080
I'm already dying of thirst.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:40.300
Well, we have lots of water, right?

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.100
You can't drink seawater.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:42.990
Huh? Why?

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.200
Because there's salt in it.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.640
You'll lose more moisture than you take in.

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:52.670
Let's say that a person
needs two liters of water a day.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:55.250
If you lose two percent of that, you get thirsty.

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:57.740
At three percent, signs of dehydration start.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:03:59.970
At five percent, you get
 headaches and dizziness.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:02.580
At ten percent, organ function decreases...

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:03.760
leading to death.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.010
You really know a lot about this.

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:09.530
What's all this?

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:11.520
H-Hey!

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.440
You shouldn't be moving yet...

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:21.320
A... A fish?

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:22.700
It's a flying fish.

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:24.490
Huh?

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.540
You're not... Are you going to eat it?

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:30.490
What we need right now is moisture.

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:34.380
Digesting food uses a lot
 of moisture too, so...

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:36.170
It's better not to eat for now.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:38.540
Wrap it in fabric and crush it well...

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.170
then when you squeeze it like this...

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:49.190
You can drink the bodily fluids, like the
moisture from the organs and the blood.

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:50.430
Here. You should have some, too.

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:55.730
<b>Are We Lost?</b>

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.730
Really...?

00:04:56.430 --> 00:05:00.820
If there are flying fish...
that means there's land nearby.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:04.610
I can't see anything besides the horizon.

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:08.400
When you're on the water, 
the horizon you see isn't very far away.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.950
It's probably about four
 or five kilometers ahead.

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:13.330
I'm amazed you can still wear that shirt.

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:17.370
The circumference of the Earth
is about 40,000 kilometers, so...

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:20.220
The radius would be about 6,400 kilometers.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:23.800
If you put our view point
at just over one meter, then...

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:26.840
According to the Pythagorean theorem...

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:31.900
It's true. What we're seeing is only
 about four or five kilometers away.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:33.990
Wow! You're super smart!

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:36.570
I guess I shouldn't have expected
any less from someone wearing glasses!

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:38.600
G-Glasses have nothing to do with it.

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:42.030
Forget that, we need water! Water!

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:45.490
Unlike that fish juice girl,
the rest of us are delicate!

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:47.820
How thirsty are you?

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:49.410
I'm dying of thirst!

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:52.120
I see. All right, then.

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:52.740
Huh?

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:55.520
Close your eyes, open your mouth,
 and lie down facing up.

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:56.950
Why?

00:05:58.230 --> 00:05:59.830
To help remove resistance to the idea.

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:01.250
Don't worry.

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:03.310
When it's fresh, it's sterile.

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:05.750
What are you guys doing?

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:06.670
Oh, I missed.

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:09.290
Wait, please! Not that!

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:10.470
It's better than dying, isn't it?

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:12.310
She's still fine! Probably!

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:13.170
But...

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:15.140
Do I have to keep waiting? Hurry up!

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:18.140
<b>Are You Lost?</b>

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:17.330
I see.

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:27.030
A rescue team... never came for us.

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:29.860
Our one and only class trip is...

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:32.530
Why did it turn out like this?

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:36.890
If nothing had gone wrong,
we'd be in bed at the hotel right now.

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:41.710
Does anyone have any fun stories?

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:46.070
I guess not, right?

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:01.590
Hey. Hey!

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:06.380
Is it morning?

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:07.820
You're finally awake.

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:13.100
Hey, I can see land.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:13.780
What?

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:14.780
Over there.

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:20.960
But with these currents,
 we're going to miss it.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:24.360
Dying would be better than
having to stay here forever.

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:26.000
We have no choice but to swim.

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:28.760
That is the most reasonable choice.

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:31.420
A tiger shark?

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:32.830
It couldn't be worse.

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:34.550
What's wrong?

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:36.860
It's nothing. Just get swimming.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:43.590
It's pretty far, isn't it?

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:46.520
It's just a little further! Keep—

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:49.440
Sh...

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:51.400
Shark!

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:53.730
Damn.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:15.880
I kept using an old-style phone
in case of disasters, but...

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:19.050
Well, there's no helping it now.

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:20.250
Is everyone all right?

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:23.560
I was so scared...

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:25.190
I can't take any more.

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:26.180
Where's the shark?

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:27.820
It swam off.

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:30.800
Sharks have special receptors
in their noses, the ampullae of Lorenzini,

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:32.240
that can sense electricity.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:36.740
You can deliver a pretty severe
shock using a cell phone battery.

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:39.290
It's amazing you know that.

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:41.790
I learned from my father.

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:47.050
Hey, is this place really safer... for us?

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:52.130
<b>Are You Lost?</b>

00:08:49.690 --> 00:08:51.250
Yes!

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:55.680
<b>– Now –</b>

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:56.500
Hey! We're not done with introductions!

00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:59.710
What do you mean, this isn't
the first time you've been stranded?

00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:00.350
Oh.

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:03.740
Before, in the Pacific Ocean,
the Atlantic Ocean, the Indian Ocean...

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:06.980
While I was traveling with my father
we ran into a few problems.

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:08.370
Right.

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:12.160
So... I guess you're sort of a pro
at getting stranded, Onishima-san.

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:13.690
What do we do now?

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:16.350
What is essential in a survival situation is—

00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:18.980
Amatani-san! Are you all right?

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:21.030
We need to carry her to the shade, gently.

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:25.210
I'm sorry...

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:26.120
Don't worry about it.

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:28.330
Her pulse is normal.

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:30.250
It's most likely dehydration.

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:33.450
You should rest here
in the shade without moving.

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:35.840
First, we need to find drinking water.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:39.770
Please gather up any empty bottles
you find washed up on the beach.

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:41.180
I'll go, too.

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:43.930
I probably have the most
endurance of anyone here.

00:09:43.930 --> 00:09:45.450
Wh-What's that about?

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:48.890
You two are going to look for water,
and we have to pick up trash?

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:52.110
To secure large amounts of water,
we will need receptacles.

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:55.490
And it would be best
if someone stayed with her.

00:09:57.430 --> 00:10:00.630
About finding water. Allow me
to give a simple explanation.

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:02.530
Hey. Did you eat something just now?

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:05.870
There are five ways to obtain drinking water.

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:08.170
First, to take it from plants or animals.

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:12.360
You can get water from
coconuts and cut banana trunks.

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:16.260
Though it doesn't seem like
this island would have either.

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:18.490
You can get water by cutting
the bamboo and vines

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:20.450
that are more likely to be here, but...

00:10:20.450 --> 00:10:22.640
it takes a really long time.

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:25.230
The second way is to purify undrinkable water.

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:27.820
The third way is to distill seawater.

00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:30.080
But we don't have the tools for either.

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:32.480
The fourth way is rain water, but...

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:35.650
We can't wait around for rain to fall.

00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:37.960
The fifth way is to find a water source.

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:42.370
Given the size of this island,
there should be a spring somewhere.

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:45.140
Just wait a little longer.

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:48.790
I-I've been feeling lightheaded for a while.

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:52.250
Collect your spit in your mouth
and breathe through your nose.

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:56.420
I don't have any spit in my mouth!

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:04.100
Don't worry. I gave you some of mine.

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:07.390
Hey! Stay with us!

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:09.480
H-Homare-san...

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:12.600
W-What... is this?

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:14.770
Oh.

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:16.180
It's a leg.

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:18.250
From the locust I ate earlier.

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:23.660
My first kiss... tasted of locust.

00:12:25.770 --> 00:12:27.510
Next time, Case 2:

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:29.080
"Our First Meal."

00:12:27.970 --> 00:12:29.970
<b>Our First Meal</b>
